in response to its violent invasion of  ukraine western nations have imposed a  series of powerful penalties on russia  and those penalties have started to hit  the russian economy hard  here's how sanctions and other measures  are rippling through russia affecting  everyone from president vladimir putin  to everyday russian citizens  first let's rewind to the beginning of  the conflict when putin first announced  a military operation in ukraine the west  responded with a first round of  penalties including blacklisting some  russian banks and halting the nordstream  2 pipeline those first moves sent a  message but the russian ruble and  russian markets were relatively stable  then as russian forces closed in on kiev  the us the eu and other western allies  ramped up the pressure they sanctioned  putin himself and other russian  officials freezing their assets overseas  then as the violence across ukraine  escalated and ukrainian president  vlodimir zielinski pleaded for more help  the u.s and its allies imposed the most  powerful penalties to date they  sanctioned russia's central bank making  it hard for it to sell its foreign  currency reserves in other words the  sanctions prevent it from using its  rainy day fund to prop up the ruble and  those countries agreed to cut some of  russia's private banks off from the  swift global payment network why do  these moves matter because they  effectively stop the flow of money into  and out of russia that cripples russian  banks but it also severely limits  moscow's ability to manage the economy  and cushion the blow that third round of  sanctions sent the russian ruble  plunging and on monday it had one of its  biggest one-day drops ever nervous  citizens were photographed lining up at  banks and atms to withdraw cash a weaker  currency means a lower standard of  living and anything that's imported into  russia like phones and electronics are  about to get a lot more expensive and  that's why the spiraling ruble is  expected to stoke inflation which was  already high at 8.7 percent in russia in  january one former russian central bank  official told the journal that inflation  could rise by five percentage points so  if russians step on the breaks and stop  spending as much on goods and services  you could see factories close and  businesses shudder and the entire  russian economy shrink of course the  russian government doesn't want any of  this it wants to limit the impact of  sanctions so it has responded by  imposing capital controls it temporarily  closed the moscow stock exchange  preventing anyone from selling stocks it  also prohibited citizens from moving  their money to banks outside of the  country and the bank of russia doubled  its benchmark interest rate hiking it  from 9.5 percent to 20 on monday  the goal of that move is to encourage  people to put their money into russian  banks where they'll earn more interest  because russian banks have all but been  cut off from the rest of the world but  higher interest rates aren't necessarily  a good thing for russian citizens and  businesses higher interest rates mean  it's going to cost a lot more to borrow  money plus dramatic moves by a central  bank tend to intensify fears of a  financial crisis beyond sanctions  western companies have begun cutting  ties with russia for example apple  stopped all product sales in the country  disney halted the release of movies  there us venues pulled russian alcohol  and volvo stopped doing business in  russia russian businesses and citizens  are also facing other consequences due  to this war like restrictions on travel  because russian planes are now banned  from flying in a number of countries  russian officials have acknowledged the  financial pain but there's no sign that  the sanctions will force president putin  to pull his troops out of ukraine which  of course is the ultimate goal as he  criticized the sanctions monday putin  called the u.s and its allies an empire  of lies  so do you think the u.s and its allies  should go even further with sanctions  leave me a comment below and if you  found this video informative be sure to  subscribe to the channel i'll see you  next time  you
 

